LFWorld and WebShop. L43:       2. cite33†Search QA. L44:       3. cite34†Comparison methods and sources. L45:     2. cite35†B.2 Training Configuration L46:       1. cite36†Actor and writer. L47:       2. cite37†Rollout batches and sequence limits. L48:       3. cite38†Writer decoding. L49:       4. cite39†Optimization and actor decoding. L50:       5. cite40†OPD weighting and loss aggregation. L51:     3. cite41†B.3 Evaluation Protocol L52:       1. cite42†WebShop decoding and checkpoint selection. L53:       2. cite43†Search QA evaluation. L54:   10. cite44†C Additional Analysis Experiments L55:     1. cite45†C.1 Regional Alignment Diagnostic L56:       1. cite46†Rollout collection and sample composition. L57:       2. cite47†Outcome returns and action pooling. L58:       3. cite48†Filtering and OPD comparison. L59:     2. cite49†C.2 Controlled Recovery Protocol L60: cite50†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L61: \nL62: arXiv:2610.23048v1 [cs.AI] 16 Oct 2026\nL63: # Distill Until Divergence:\nL64: Error-Truncated Hindsight Distillation for Agentic RL\nL65: \nL66: Yibin Huang Affiliation: Faculty of Computing, Harbin Institute of Technology    Conghui Zhu ^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author. Affiliation: Faculty of Computing, Harbin Institute of Technology\nL67: ###### Abstract\nL68: Long-horizon interaction requires agents to make multiple decisions in a changing environment. Outcome-based GRPO lacks fine-grained guidance for intermediate decisions. Recent methods distill completed trajectories into reusable skills and use hindsight-conditioned OPD to provide dense token-level credit.\nL69: However, skill-induced probability shifts do not necessarily represent reward-aligned credit; especially after an erroneous action changes subsequent interaction states, skill-conditioned supervision may become mismatched with the current state.\nL70: To address this mismatch, we propose ETRO, which routes two complementary hindsight skills according to trajectory outcomes and error boundaries: Positive skills capture successful workflows to reinforce successful trajectories and pre-error decisions, while Negative skills capture failure-specific corrections to target decisive errors. The post-error suffix is optimized exclusively by GRPO, retaining outcome-based learning without extending potentially mismatched hindsight supervision.\nL71: Extensive experiments demonstrate competitive task performance and support effective fine-grained credit allocation.\nL72: ## 1 Introduction\nL73: \nL74: Large language model agents are increasingly used for tasks that require sustained interaction with external tools and environments, including web navigation (cite51†Wei et al., 2025 ), household tasks (cite52†Shridhar et al., 2020 ), and search-augmented reasoning (cite53†Huang et al., 2026 ). These applications extend language models beyond single-response generation toward long-horizon task execution, where each action can alter the observations and choices available in subsequent turns.\nL75: Reinforcement learning provides a natural way to learn these strategies from interaction outcomes, but outcome-based GRPO lacks explicit guidance for intermediate decisions (cite54†Shao et al., 2024 ). Recent methods supplement it with trajectory-derived skills, using on-policy distillation (OPD) to provide token-level credit (cite55†Yang et al., 2026b ; cite56†Wu et al., 2026 ).\nL76: Yet denser credit is not necessarily better-aligned credit: after an error changes the interaction state, the same skill may no longer guide subsequent decisions appropriately. Extending its supervision over the suffix can therefore introduce state-mismatched updates (cite57†Tan et al., 2026 ; cite58†Qu et al., 2026 ).\nL77: Our view is that hindsight should inform selective credit allocation. We refer to guidance extending beyond the states and decisions it meaningfully describes as hindsight applicability mismatch. A skill that explains a completed trajectory need not be equally applicable to every decision along it. An unrecovered decisive error can separate workflow consolidation, local correction, and outcome-based learning.\nL78: We propose ETRO to route two functionally distinct hindsight skills. A Positive skill summarizes a successful workflow and guides OPD throughout successful trajectories and over the pre-error prefix of failed trajectories. A Negative skill summarizes failure-specific corrections and guides OPD only at the writer-estimated first unrecovered decisive error. The post-error suffix remains optimized exclusively by GRPO, removing potentially mismatched auxiliary credit without discarding outcome-based learning.\nL79: By integrating skill-conditioned OPD into the GRPO objective, ETRO combines targeted hindsight guidance with outcome-based learning without requiring skills during task execution.\nL80: We evaluate ETRO on WebShop, ALFWorld, and Search QA using Qwen2.5 actors from 1.5B to 7B parameters. At 7B, ETRO achieves 85.8% WebShop success, 97.0% ALFWorld success, and 51.8% Search QA macro-average accuracy. Ablation studies further support the effectiveness of its selective credit allocation.\nL81: \nL82: Our contributions are threefold:\nL83: \nL84:   * •\nL85: \nL86: We formulate hindsight applicability mismatch: skill-induced probability shifts need not provide reward-aligned credit after an error changes the interaction state.\nL87:   * •\nL88: \nL89: We introduce ETRO, which separates workflow guidance from failure-specific correction and routes their OPD credit by trajectory region, while retaining outcome-based GRPO over the complete rollout.\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: \nL93: Extensive experiments across three agentic domains demonstrate ETRO’s effectiveness, with ablation studies validating its selective credit allocation.\nL94: ## 2 Related Work\nL95: #### Reinforcement Learning with Outcome Rewards.\nL96: Outcome-reward reinforcement learning optimizes policies from completed-trajectory feedback without requiring intermediate annotations, making it suitable for tasks with automatically verifiable outcomes. PPO stabilizes policy-gradient updates through clipping and a learned value function that estimates expected returns (cite59†Schulman et al., 2017 ). ReMax uses a rollout-derived baseline to reduce the need for a separately trained critic (cite60†Li et al., 2023 ).\nL97: RLOO estimates advantages by comparing each sampled response with a leave-one-out baseline from other responses (cite61†Ahmadian et al., 2024 ). GRPO derives group-relative advantages from verifiable rewards within a sampling group rather than a learned value model (cite54†Shao et al., 2024 ). DAPO improves the stability and efficiency of group-based policy optimization (cite62†Yu et al., 2026 ).\nL98: Search-R1 extends outcome-driven training to agents that interleave reasoning and search, using answer-correctness rewards (cite63†Jin et al., 2025 ). WebAgent-R1 trains interactive web agents using task-success feedback across multi-step interactions (cite51†Wei et al., 2025 ). Rather than changing the outcome-reward estimator, ETRO retains GRPO and supplements trajectory-level feedback with auxiliary hindsight credit routed according to the position and role of intermediate decisions.\nL99: #### On-Policy Hindsight and Skill Distillation.\nL100: On-policy hindsight converts post-rollout information into dense supervision on current-policy samples, using completed experience to guide subsequent policy updates. OPSD constructs self-teachers for distillation on the policy’s own sampled responses (cite64†Zhao et al., 2026a ). SDPO conditions self-teachers on feedback to improve supervision of those responses (cite65†Hübotter et al., 2026 ). RLSD constrains hindsight likelihood shifts when modulating reward-driven updates (cite66†Yang et al., 2026a ).\nL101: Skill-SD summarizes interaction experience as natural-language skills for actor distillation (cite67†Wang et al., 2026 ). OPID uses trajectory-derived skills as privileged context for on-policy distillation (cite55†Yang et al., 2026b ). SEED distills skills extracted from completed experience into the agent (cite56†Wu et al., 2026 ). TRIAL calibrates hindsight signals through trajectory-relative normalization (cite68†Zheng et al., 2026 ). ATOD regulates distillation using uncertainty (cite57†Tan et al., 2026 ).\nL102: ICSD calibrates supervision by its influence on the optimization objective (cite69†Lan et al., 2026 ). TurnSight assesses turn-level supervision through lookahead agreement (cite58†Qu et al., 2026 ). ETRO instead distinguishes the temporal applicability of workflow and failure-diagnostic skills, routing them before and at a diagnosed error while disabling auxiliary OPD on the subsequent suffix. ETRO thus addresses skill applicability, not only supervision strength.\nL103: Truncating auxiliary OPD leaves outcome-based learning active on the suffix.\nL104: Figure 1: OPD signals versus GiGPO-based step advantages on Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct failed trajectories. Each point represents one interaction turn.\nL105: #### Localized Supervision and Path Divergence.\nL106: Localized supervision provides finer-grained evidence about intermediate decisions than terminal rewards alone, distinguishing useful progress from errors within trajectories that share the same final outcome. Math-Shepherd estimates step correctness by sampling continuations from intermediate reasoning states and uses these estimates to train a process reward model (cite70†Wang et al., 2024 ).\nL107: OmegaPRM applies divide-and-conquer search to locate the first erroneous reasoning step, turning outcome information into a localized process label (cite71†Luo et al., 2024 ). ROSD uses reflection to identify an erroneous span and directs self-distillation toward that span rather than uniformly supervising the entire response (cite72†Zhao et al., 2026b ).\nL108: HSD conditions on a successful sibling trajectory to concentrate supervision near the point where successful and unsuccessful paths diverge (cite73†Li et al., 2026 ). Beyond localizing supervision, ETRO partitions failed trajectories at a diagnosed unrecovered error into Positive-guided prefixes, Negative-guided error turns, and GRPO-only suffixes.\nL109: ## 3 Method\nL110: \nL111: ### 3.1 Overview\nL112: \nL113: The remainder of this section is organized as follows. Section cite13†3.2 formalizes the multi-turn agent setting, and Section cite14†3.3 presents the empirical observations that motivate our design. Section cite15†3.4 then introduces the ETRO algorithm.\nL114: \nL115: cite74†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of ETRO’s trajectory-aware routing of Positive and Negative hindsight credit.\nL116: ### 3.2 Problem Formulation\nL117: Let $\\mathcal{D}$ be a collection of tasks and $\\mathcal{E}$ an interactive environment or external tool. Given a task $x\\sim\\mathcal{D}$ and an initial observation $o_{1}$, the agent interacts with $\\mathcal{E}$ to produce a trajectory $\\tau=(x,o_{1},u_{1},\\ldots,u_{T})$, where $T$ is the number of interaction turns.\nL118: Each turn $u_{t}=(y_{t},a_{t},o_{t+1})$ consists of the model-generated response $y_{t}$, the executable action $a_{t}$ extracted from that response, and the resulting environment observation $o_{t+1}$. The response contains reasoning and a task-specific action or final answer, generated by the policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$ conditioned on the task and preceding interaction history. The trajectory ends with a final answer, environment completion, or exhaustion of the turn budget.\nL119: A task-specific verifier assigns a binary training outcome indicating success or failure. For trajectory $i$, we use $\\tau_{i}$ and $T_{i}$ to denote the trajectory and its number of turns, and $y_{i,t,j}$ to denote token $j$ of the model response at turn $t$.\nL120: ### 3.3 Preliminary Study: Regional Alignment of OPD Credit\nL121: We investigate whether the alignment between skill-induced OPD signals and outcome-based action quality varies across trajectory regions. A GRPO-trained Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct actor samples eight complete trajectories per task prompt. The writer summarizes reusable workflows from successful and failed trajectories as Positive skills and failure-specific corrections as Negative skills.\nL122: On failed trajectories, we quantify each skill’s influence by averaging the difference between skill-conditioned and unconditioned log-probabilities over each turn’s reasoning and action tokens. We assess alignment through Spearman correlations with step-level advantages derived from GiGPO’s state–action grouping approach (cite75†Feng et al., 2026 ), separately before, at, and after the diagnosed error.\nL123: These advantages compare pooled action returns against a shared-state baseline; estimation and filtering details appear in Appendix cite45†C.1 .\nL124: OPD modulation shows region-dependent correlations with the action-pooled reference advantage, with stronger positive associations in the prefix for Positive skills and at the diagnosed error for Negative skills than in the post-error suffix. As shown in Figure cite76†1 , Positive modulation has a correlation of $0.59$ in the prefix but $0.01$ in the suffix; Negative modulation has a correlation of $0.46$ at the diagnosed error but $-0.05$ in the suffix.\nL125: The weak suffix correlations suggest that, after an error changes the interaction state, skill-induced probability shifts may no longer align with outcome-based action quality. This potential mismatch motivates testing Positive guidance before the error and Negative guidance at the error, while removing suffix OPD to avoid extending potentially mismatched skill supervision and retaining GRPO to learn from the trajectory’s actual outcome.\nL126: ### 3.4 Error-Truncated Polarity-Routed Distillation\nL127: Figure cite77†2 illustrates how ETRO routes hindsight supervision using a writer-estimated error boundary. For each completed trajectory $\\tau_{i}$, the writer produces a Positive skill $z_{i}^{+}$ that summarizes reusable task-solving strategies to reinforce effective decisions through OPD.\nL128: For a failed trajectory, it also identifies the earliest turn $k_{i}$ whose erroneous decision is judged decisive for the observed failure and remains uncorrected by later actions, and produces a Negative skill $z_{i}^{-}$ describing the decision, its consequence, and an executable alternative. This boundary separates Positive-guided decisions before the error from the local Negative correction at the error and the GRPO-only suffix.\nL129: Failed trajectories without a valid boundary retain Positive guidance throughout; we represent this case by $k_{i}=\\infty$. Writer prompts, boundary validation, and skill construction details appear in Appendix cite27†A .\nL130: Let $c_{i,t,j}$ denote the original generation context of token $y_{i,t,j}$, comprising the task, preceding interaction history, and earlier tokens in the current response. Let $p\\in\\{+,-\\}$ index the skill polarity, with $z_{i}^{p}$ denoting the corresponding skill. We compute the OPD gap:\nL131: \nL132:  | $\\displaystyle\\Delta^{p}_{i,t,j}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}(y_{i,t,j}\\mid c_{i,t,j},z_{i}^{p})$  |  | (1)\nL133:  |  | $\\displaystyle-\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}(y_{i,t,j}\\mid c_{i,t,j}),$  |\nL134: Here, $\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}$ is the frozen rollout policy, and $\\Delta^{p}_{i,t,j}$ is fixed during optimization. Skills provide additional context only for re-scoring and are absent during rollout and evaluation.\nL135: \nL136: The unweighted routed advantage for each eligible reasoning or action token is\nL137:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{OPD}}_{i,t,j}=\\begin{cases}\\Delta^{+}_{i,t,j},&\\text{if $\\tau_{i}$ succeeds},\\\\\nL138: \\Delta^{+}_{i,t,j},&\\text{if $\\tau_{i}$ fails and $t<k_{i}$},\\\\\nL139: \\Delta^{-}_{i,t,j},&\\text{if $\\tau_{i}$ fails and $t=k_{i}$},\\\\\nL140: 0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL141: \nL142: We weight this auxiliary advantage by $\\lambda$ and add it to the trajectory-level GRPO advantage:\nL143: \nL144:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{ETRO}}_{i,t,j}=A_{i}^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}+\\lambda A^{\\mathrm{OPD}}_{i,t,j}.$$  |  | (3)\nL145: Here, $A_{i}^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}$ is the group-relative outcome advantage of trajectory $i$, and $\\lambda$ controls the strength of both OPD branches. We substitute $A^{\\mathrm{ETRO}}_{i,t,j}$ into the standard clipped GRPO objective, setting the OPD term to zero outside eligible reasoning and action spans. Post-error truncation therefore removes only auxiliary supervision; GRPO continues to optimize every valid response token in the complete rollout.\nL146: ## 4 Experiments\nL147: Table 1: Results on ALFWorld, Search QA, and WebShop across Qwen2.5-Instruct model scales (%). Higher values indicate better performance.\nL148: Method  | ALFWorld  | Search QA  | WebShop\nL149:  | Succ.  | NQ  | Triv  | Pop  | Hotp  | 2Wk  | MuS  | Bam  | Avg.  | Score  | Succ.\nL150: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\nL151: ---\nL152: GRPO  | 65.7  | 16.6  | 34.1  | 15.9  | 16.1  | 24.8  | 3.4  | 58.1  | 24.1  | 72.0  | 62.0\nL153: GRPO+OPSD  | 71.1  | 16.9  | 34.3  | 16.4  | 16.3  | 24.8  | 3.4  | 56.9  | 24.1  | 84.9  | 73.4\nL154: Skill-SD  | 68.0  | 16.7  | 35.4  | 14.8  | 16.7  | 25.2  | 3.9  | 11.2  | 17.7  | 80.6  | 68.8\nL155: RLSD  | 67.2  | 13.9  | 29.4  | 12.4  | 14.2  | 21.1  | 3.0  | 9.5  | 14.8  | 80.5  | 63.3\nL156: SDAR  | 72.7  | 16.1  | 32.7  | 15.8  | 15.9  | 24.4  | 3.3  | 4.4  | 16.1  | 81.9  | 64.1\nL157: ICSD  | 73.4  | 18.3  | 39.2  | 17.1  | 18.7  | 28.2  | 4.4  | 68.0  | 27.7  | 85.6  | 75.8\nL158: ETRO  | 79.1  | 19.4  | 40.8  | 18.7  | 20.1  | 30.4  | 5.2  | 69.8  | 29.2  | 87.9  | 80.8\nL159: Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct\nL160: ---\nL161: GRPO  | 75.0  | 39.3  | 60.6  | 41.1  | 37.4  | 34.6  | 15.4  | 26.4  | 36.4  | 79.8  | 64.4\nL162: GRPO+OPSD  | 81.2  | 44.9  | 61.2  | 45.2  | 40.4  | 38.5  | 16.0  | 66.1  | 44.6  | 77.8  | 66.4\nL163: Skill-SD  | 73.4  | 44.4  | 60.4  | 44.0  | 39.5  | 40.4  | 15.4  | 64.9  | 44.1  | 75.9  | 64.0\nL164: RLSD  | 79.7  | 41.5  | 58.6  | 42.3  | 40.4  | 40.2  | 16.8  | 66.9  | 43.8  | 84.4  | 66.4\nL165: SDAR  | 84.4  | 44.8  | 58.1  | 44.3  | 38.6  | 36.2  | 15.7  | 66.1  | 43.4  | 85.0  | 68.0\nL166: OPID  | 84.3  | 45.9  | 61.4  | 45.7  | 40.7  | 38.8  | 16.4  | 66.1  | 45.0  | 85.0  | 74.2\nL167: SEED  | 91.8  | 44.3  | 61.9  | 47.2  | 41.0  | 42.0  | 16.9  | 67.7  | 45.9  | 88.5  | 78.9\nL168: ETRO  | 93.3  | 47.2  | 63.8  | 48.5  | 43.2  | 44.1  | 18.7  | 70.8  | 48.0  | 90.3  | 83.0\nL169: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\nL170: ---\nL171: GRPO  | 81.2  | 45.1  | 63.7  | 44.0  | 43.6  | 43.2  | 16.8  | 37.6  | 42.0  | 80.9  | 73.7\nL172: GRPO+OPSD  | 80.4  | 47.3  | 64.5  | 46.9  | 43.8  | 39.3  | 18.0  | 69.4  | 47.0  | 86.8  | 76.5\nL173: Skill-SD  | 85.1  | 47.1  | 64.5  | 47.8  | 44.2  | 42.1  | 20.2  | 69.0  | 47.8  | 86.1  | 76.5\nL174: RLSD  | 82.0  | 46.8  | 63.0  | 44.4  | 45.5  | 48.9  | 21.5  | 73.0  | 49.0  | 87.4  | 77.3\nL175: SDAR  | 85.9  | 46.3  | 63.5  | 48.2  | 43.8  | 48.4  | 19.6  | 73.0  | 49.0  | 89.4  | 82.8\nL176: OPID  | 90.0  | 48.8  | 65.6  | 46.8  | 46.1  | 42.7  | 21.7  | 72.6  | 49.2  | 85.3  | 79.7\nL177: SEED  | 96.1  | 47.0  | 64.9  | 47.8  | 45.2  | 45.3  | 20.1  | 70.2  | 48.6  | 89.7  | 78.1\nL178: ETRO  | 97.0  | 50.4  | 67.1  | 49.5  | 48.0  | 50.2  | 23.1  | 74.0  | 51.8  | 91.7  | 85.8\nL179: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL180: \nL181: #### Benchmarks and metrics.\nL182: \nL183: We evaluate ETRO on WebShop (cite78†Yao et al., 2022 ), ALFWorld (cite52†Shridhar et al., 2020 ), and Search QA. We report normalized score and exact success for WebShop, success rate for ALFWorld, and average answer accuracy across the seven Search QA datasets.\nL184: #### Models and baselines.\nL185: We train Qwen2.5-Instruct models at 1.5B, 3B, and 7B scales and compare against GRPO, GRPO+OPSD, Skill-SD, RLSD, SDAR, OPID (cite55†Yang et al., 2026b ), SEED (cite56†Wu et al., 2026 ), and ICSD (cite69†Lan et al., 2026 ). In Table cite79†1 , ETRO results come from our experiments, whereas all baseline results are taken from prior reports rather than locally reproduced under a unified protocol. Skills are absent during ETRO evaluation.\nL186: Result provenance and our training and evaluation settings are described in Appendix cite30†B .\nL187: #### Training configuration.\nL188: \nL189: We sample eight rollouts per task and optimize with AdamW at learning rate $10^{-6}$, using one PPO epoch per batch and clipping parameter 0.2. A frozen Qwen3.5-27B writer supplies skills, with OPD coefficient $\\lambda=0.001$.\nL190: #### Evaluation protocol.\nL191: \nL192: WebShop evaluation covers 500 tasks and ALFWorld uses 134 Unseen tasks, both with a 15-turn limit. Search QA follows the Search-R1 splits and reports normalized exact-match accuracy averaged equally across seven datasets.\nL193: Table 2: Skill selection and branch combination on WebShop and ALFWorld using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL194: Method  | WebShop  | ALFWorld\nL195: --- | --- | ---\nL196: Succ.  | Score  | Succ.\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: GRPO  | 62.0  | 72.0  | 65.7\nL199: Global Positive  | 77.2  | 85.3  | 76.9\nL200: Global Negative  | 68.8  | 75.3  | 72.4\nL201: Global Both  | 76.0  | 83.8  | 76.9\nL202: Positive Only  | 77.6  | 85.0  | 77.6\nL203: Negative Only  | 73.4  | 79.8  | 74.6\nL204: ETRO  | 80.8  | 87.9  | 79.1\nL205: Table 3: Suffix supervision and boundary selection on WebShop and ALFWorld using Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.\nL206: Method  | WebShop  | ALFWorld\nL207: --- | --- | ---\nL208: Succ.  | Score  | Succ.\nL209: --- | --- | ---\nL210: GRPO  | 62.0  | 72.0  | 65.7\nL211: Suffix Positive  | 77.6  | 84.6  | 77.6\nL212: Suffix Negative  | 72.2  | 79.3  | 74.6\nL213: Random Boundary  | 72.8  | 79.5  | 73.1\nL214: ETRO  | 80.8  | 87.9  | 79.1\nL215: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL216: \nL217: Compared with GRPO and its self-distillation extension GRPO+OPSD, ETRO improves performance across all three domains and model scales in Table cite79†1 . At 1.5B, its WebShop success reaches 80.8%, exceeding GRPO by 18.8 percentage points. At 3B, its Search QA macro-average accuracy reaches 48.0%, surpassing GRPO+OPSD by 3.4 points.\nL218: Compared with related distillation methods, ETRO also achieves the highest reported results among the listed methods at each scale. At 1.5B, its WebShop and ALFWorld success rates exceed ICSD by 5.0 and 5.7 points; at 3B, they exceed SEED by 4.1 and 1.5 points, respectively. At 7B, ETRO improves over SDAR’s WebShop success by 3.0 points and OPID’s Search QA macro-average accuracy by 2.6 points.\nL219: These gains extend beyond outcome-only training to existing distillation approaches across interactive task execution and search-augmented reasoning.\nL220: ### 4.3 Ablation Studies\nL221: Skill Selection and Branch Combination. To assess skill selection and branch combination, we organize the Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct comparisons on WebShop and ALFWorld into five groups (Table cite80†3 ): GRPO without OPD; Global Positive and Global Negative with one skill applied throughout the trajectory; Global Both with both skills combined globally; Positive Only and Negative Only with one branch removed from ETRO’s routing rule; and the complete ETRO method.\nL222: Positive Only retains ETRO’s Positive guidance on successful trajectories, failed-trajectory prefixes, and complete failed trajectories without a valid boundary, while Negative Only retains its Negative guidance at diagnosed errors. All OPD variants use the same coefficient $\\lambda=0.001$ and retain GRPO over the complete rollout; branch removal only changes the coverage of auxiliary supervision.\nL223: The complete method outperforms all variants on the three reported metrics, reaching 80.8% WebShop success and 79.1% ALFWorld success. On WebShop, it exceeds Global Positive and Global Negative by 3.6 and 12.0 percentage points. Removing the Negative or Positive branch lowers WebShop success to 77.6% or 73.4% and ALFWorld success to 77.6% or 74.6%, respectively, supporting the complementary roles of workflow guidance and local correction.\nL224: Global Both averages the two skill-induced gaps throughout failed trajectories while retaining Positive guidance on successful trajectories. Its WebShop success of 76.0% remains below ETRO, suggesting that combining skills globally is less effective than routing them by trajectory region.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgentic In-Context Learning: What Algorithms Can Transformers Learn in Closed-Loop Interaction? (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23051v1)\nciteturn41221view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23051v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1027\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNot All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence:Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1)\nciteturn41221view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 472\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCausal Edit Serialization (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22820v1)\nciteturn41221view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22820v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1228\n"
